Wjì3%&í`FÒÊ=Ã¥ÈG7}5’Ro1ž\7´{"url":"/gcsl/implementation/pipeline/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/implementation/pipeline/","content":"100 The Language Pipeline. GCSL is a tree-walking interpreter with a typed intermediate representation. The stages are separate so a formatter or editor can stop before execution while using the same syntax and type rules as the CLI. The main pipeline is: CopySourceFile -> lexer and interned names/strings -> parser and arena-based syntax tree -> workspace resolution and type-check side tables -> type-specialized intermediate representation -> evaluation and a value with warnings The lexer and parser can recover enough structure to report multiple errors. A failed check is never lowered into executable code. Recovery supports diagnostics and editor queries; it does not make a partially valid program executable. The checker resolves names and types expressions, recording captures, slots, argument destinations, and required awaits in side tables. Lowering makes those awaits explicit. Evaluation dispatches through specialized operations whose types have already been checked, rather than repeating the type system at each operation. 100.1 Workspace scheduling # The workspace scanner merges public field paths, resolves imports, and orders source files by dependencies. Each public leaf has an owner and a cell. Reading an interior record can require all its contributing owners. A cycle is a diagnostic, not an order selected at runtime. Top-level field scheduling differs from ordinary block order. It supports forward references while ensuring dependencies are initialized before a read. Shadowed declarations are still checked even when they do not contribute a runtime value. 100.2 Sources # The principal implementation is in crates/gcsl/src/lexer.rs, parser.rs, workspace.rs, check.rs, lower.rs, and eval.rs. Each module documents its internal invariants. The implementation here is not the separate Tree-sitter grammar used 